hi and welcome to this open cue tutorial  today I'm gonna walk through submitting  a render job to open cue and go over  some of the functionality in the cue GUI  monitoring application here we are  inside Maya and we're all ready to kick  off a render of our robot dance party  scene we've set up our Maya render  settings this render is going to use  v-ray and now we'll jump over to our  render cam just to make sure everything  looks good once we're ready to submit  the job we can switch to the open cue  shelf and click on the submitter shelf  button this will launch the open cue  submission form as a Maya plugin this UI  can also be launched as a standalone  application from the command line the  first thing that we're gonna do is enter  a job name and then we'll give it a shot  name and then we'll enter a layer name  in this case lighting then we'll select  our render cam if we don't select one it  will just use the camera from our render  settings and then we'll input the frame  spec we want to render so for more  information about this frame spec syntax  we can hit the question mark icon this  will give some description and examples  about the frames vak syntax next we'll  set up the job type job types define  what parameters are exposed in the  submission you I will leave it set to  Maya in this case and then services  services help us define default resource  requirements and tags for this job we'll  submit this to the Maya service and  we'll leave the min cord requirements  set to zero this allows cubot to send  the job to any size machine and since  this is just a single layer job we'll  leave the dependency set to none but  perhaps you wanted a more complex job  maybe you wanted to kick off a nuke  renderer to be dependent on this you can  hit the plus button and configure a new  nuclear that would be dependent on our  lighting layer this demo though we're  just going to do this simple single  layer Maya render and at this point we  can double check our parameters if  everything looks good  well go ahead and click Submit and we'll  get a little window pop-up that says the  jobs been submitted to open queue and at  this point we can jump over to queue GUI  so there you can see our robots job has  popped up in the job view there and this  is cute GUI the main interface for  monitoring the state of everything in  open queue it's a pie side to  application that's built on multiple  dockable widgets allowing the user to be  able to customize the interface that  they're liking and at the top we have  the job view and down below is the  monitor host view so we'll switch over  to the job details view and then we'll  double click on our job and this will  show us the hierarchy of the job going  from top to bottom first we have a job a  job is a container for a single  submission then we have a layer you can  think of a layer as a command with a  frame range to run and at the bottom is  a frame a frame is an individual  instance of that command with a single  frame or chunk to be executed by a host  so next we're gonna walk through some of  the other interfaces in queue GUI you  can open up new panels through the views  plug-in menu we'll start with opening  the shows interface this view will  display a list of shows known by open  queue and some high level stats about  that show things like the total number  of running frames and pending frames  from there we'll open up the allocations  view allocations are a grouping  mechanism for hosts generally an  allocation is defined by the facility  that the host lives in in this case  local or cloud and then there will also  be a tag these tags will be applied to  any host in that allocation by default  you can see the number of cores in each  allocation here next we'll take a look  at the subscriptions view subscriptions  exist for a show so we'll select the  show that we're interested in and it'll  list the available allocations for that  show this then defines what hosts can  run jobs for a given show all the  allocations show the  sighs usage and bursts amount you can  edit these parameters by right-clicking  on an allocation you can also edit the  show settings from this view by clicking  on the show properties button where you  can set the core requirements and do  things like enabling or disabling  booking ok well go ahead and close the  subscription view and we'll walk through  the monitor host view next well go ahead  and turn on refresh with no filter to  populate the full list of hosts and this  will tell us all sorts of information  about the rqd host active in open queue  we can open up the attributes for you to  explore this information a little closer  you can see things here like system  stats and the allocations and tags for  this host in this case cloud general and  on this other host local on assigned if  you notice only one of these machines is  running right now that's because our  dance-party job was submitted to the  local facility so only the host with  local general allocation is eligible to  run that job the one with cloud  unassigned  cannot if you need to do anything like  modify the host you can right click on  on the host and do things like change  the allocation tags delete the host lock  or unlock the host and also leave  comments that about covers the basics of  the host monitor view and next we'll  look at some more of the job  configuration views we'll start off with  the services video services our way of  classifying a job and defining some  default requirements for that job here  we have a list of services defined by  different network applications and you  can see there are default system  requirements and tags that get applied  to jobs in that service these parameters  are defaults though and they can be  overridden at job submission time or  once the job is hit the queue so we'll  we'll jump back to the job details view  to take a look at how services are  applied to a job so we'll select our job  and right click on the  we're interested in if we open up the  properties dialog we'll see something  very similar to the services for you  this information by default is all  populated from the service that was  selected at job submission time like I  previously mentioned that's just the  default though and it can be changed on  layer by layer basis if needed  so we'll go ahead and close that and it  looks like our frames are starting to  finish up there's still a number of  frames running but these green ones are  finished so we'll take a look at the log  view if you select the log and open up  the log view you'll see the render log  for that frame in this case it's a v-ray  log that includes all of the output from  that render there's a handy search  feature down here that allows us to  quickly search for a specific text in  the log you can also open up the log by  double clicking on the frame in this  case it will open in Mac then for me you  can edit this by specifying your editor  in an environment variable that about  covers the basics of the open QQ GUI  interface thanks for watching
 

